Sure! Here's a simplified version of the update function:

```javascript
// src/stateManager.js

function updateState(currentState, action) {
    switch (action.type) {
        case 'ADD_ITEM':
            return {...currentState, items: [...currentState.items, action.payload]};
        case 'REMOVE_ITEM':
            return {...currentState, items: currentState.items.filter(item => item.id !== action.payload.id)};
        default:
            return currentState;
    }
}
```

This version uses modern JavaScript syntax and spreads to ensure immutability while improving readability.
